### My answer to the prompt

Use a specified byte order rather than sending native in-memory structs. For example, define each record as **four bytes**: a 16-bit event index in big-endian order, followed by a signed 16-bit event value in big-endian two’s-complement form. With 18 events, each packet is exactly 72 bytes.

On the Zig side, encode the packet explicitly:

```zig
const EVENT_COUNT = 18;

fn putU16BE(dst: []u8, value: u16) void {
    dst[0] = @truncate(value >> 8);
    dst[1] = @truncate(value);
}

fn putI16BE(dst: []u8, value: i16) void {
    putU16BE(dst, @bitCast(value));
}

// In the send loop, after updating controllerState:
var packet: [EVENT_COUNT * 4]u8 = undefined;

for (controllerState, 0..) |value, i| {
    const offset = i * 4;
    putU16BE(packet[offset .. offset + 2], @intCast(i));
    putI16BE(packet[offset + 2 .. offset + 4], value);
}

const addr = try net.Address.parseIp4("127.0.0.1", 31415);
_ = try posix.sendto(sock, &packet, 0, &addr.any, addr.getOsSockLen());
```

On the C side, require the expected packet size, then decode each record rather than casting the receive buffer to a struct:

```c
